# Generate MD with default content
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja.md

# Convert MD to PDF
cargo run --bin agent_doc md2pdf izin-kerja.md izin-kerja.pdf

# Or directly generate PDF
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja.pdf


# Default: uses filled content (Budi Santoso, Ahmad Rizki, etc.)
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja.md

# Form mode: empty placeholders for manual filling
$env:AGENT_DOCS="form"
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja-form.md

# Alternative syntax
$env:AGENT_DOCS="#formcontent"
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja-form.pdf

# Edit .env file to enable form mode
# Uncomment: AGENT_DOCS=form

# Then run (no need to set env var manually)
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja.md

# Or override with PowerShell env var (takes precedence)
$env:AGENT_DOCS="form"
cargo run --bin agent_doc template izin-kerja izin-kerja.md

MD VIEWER On Rust 

md viewer on rust

1. Set Up Your DependenciesAdd the following lines to your Cargo.toml file to include termimad for rich text terminal formatting:toml[dependencies]
termimad = "0.31"
Use code with caution.2. Implementation CodeReplace the contents of your src/main.rs file with the following complete program. It reads a local file path passed as a command-line argument, safely loads the content, and outputs the rendered text.rustuse std::env;
use std::fs;
use std::path::Path;
use termimad::MadSkin;

fn main() {
    // 1. Collect command-line arguments
    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
    
    if args.len() < 2 {
        eprintln!("Usage: {} <path-to-markdown-file.md>", args[0]);
        std::process::exit(1);
    }

    let file_path = &args[1];

    // 2. Validate file existence
    if !Path::new(file_path).exists() {
        eprintln!("Error: File '{}' not found.", file_path);
        std::process::exit(1);
    }

    // 3. Read the Markdown file content
    match fs::read_to_string(file_path) {
        Ok(markdown_content) => {
            // 4. Create a default skin terminal renderer
            let skin = MadSkin::default();
            
            // 5. Print the beautifully rendered markdown to stdout
            skin.print_text(&markdown_content);
        }
        Err(err) => {
            eprintln!("Error reading file: {}", err);
            std::process::exit(1);
        }
    }
}
Use code with caution.3. How to Run ItCreate a dummy Markdown file named sample.md in your project root folder.Put some sample syntax inside it:markdown# Welcome to the Rust MD Viewer
This is a paragraph featuring **bold text** and *italics*.

* Item List 1
* Item List 2

> Blockquotes work natively too!
Use code with caution.Run your program via Cargo by passing the path of the sample file:bashcargo run -- sample.md
Use code with caution.Other Ecosystem AlternativesIf you require specialized functionality beyond terminal rendering, check out these alternatives:Web & HTML Generation: Use the comrak or pulldown-cmark crates to safely parse and translate raw Markdown into HTML elements.Pre-built Binaries: For complete out-of-the-box CLI application tools, look into mdr (features live-reload and Mermaid diagram extensions) or md-tui (provides full interactive Vim-style keyboard navigation).Would you like to extend this viewer to support syntax highlighting for code blocks,or prefer to transform the output into a local web-browser live preview